/E (           athematical, logical, philosophical, economical, or otherwise:  what is your favorite paradox?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [44]
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (Mine is the Ship of Theseus.  It goes something like this:)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [45]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (Every year the Athenians sail the Ship of Theseus to the island of Delos to worship their God Apollo.  After every voyage, any planks that had been worn out from the voyage are replaced by new planks.  After many years, none of the original planks remain.  What's more, it's discovered that a shipwright had been quietly saving and restoring the discarded planks and he used them to rebuild the original ship plank for plank.  Which ship is the Ship of Theseus?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [46]
>>
endobj
391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (The reason I like this one is because it asks about the meaning of identity, and the continuity of it.  It's inspired a lot of fiction, like Star Trek's transporter episodes, The Prestige, and Klara and the Sun.  It's also not an abstract logical paradox - it is easily envisioned and explained in real world terms, but it doesn't admit a clean solution.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [47]
>>
endobj
392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (311 sats \\ 6 replies \\ @Undisciplined 30 Sep)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [48]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (I have a major soft spot for Zeno's Paradoxes.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [49]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (I particularly like the framing of the foot race. How can you ever catch up to a slower runner, if every time you get to where they had been, they've moved farther along?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [50]
>>
endobj
